course: dictionary
lesson: 1094
title: One word, two jobs: braid
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Some words can be a noun (a thing) or a verb (an action). Braid is one of them, and the context tells you which job it is doing. «Their neighbour Mrs Adeyemi comes round on Sunday nights and braids her hair while Grandad makes the tea.» Here braids is a verb: she is doing something, making her hair into a braid.
explain: Other sentences use the noun form. «In Baltimore, Tremaine's dad does his cornrows (tight braids along the head) before school, badly.» The brackets explain the word: a braid is hair divided into three or more pieces and twisted over each other. Cornrows are one kind of braid, so braids here names the hairstyle itself.
explain: Braid can also mean a decorative band of woven thread, used on uniforms and curtains. Context helps again. If the sentence is about hair, string or bread, braid probably means the twisted shape. If it is about clothes or curtains, it probably means the trim.
fact: Braid can be a noun (a braid) or a verb (to braid, braids, braided, braiding).
fact: «A braid» means hair divided into three or more pieces and twisted into one long rope shape.
fact: «To braid» means to make hair, rope, string or bread into that shape.
fact: «Braid» can also mean a decorative band of woven thread, used on uniforms and curtains.
fact: US and Canada say braid most; UK, Ireland and Australia often say plait, which means the same as braid for hair.
exercise: «Mrs Adeyemi comes round on Sunday nights and braids her hair while Grandad makes the tea.» What does braids mean here? | Washes | *Makes hair into a twisted, rope-shaped plait | Cuts short | Hangs loose
exercise: Ella in Ireland says, «I'll plait my hair before school.» Does plait mean the same as braid? | No, a plait is a gold band on a uniform | *Yes, in the UK a braid is more often called a plait | No, a plait is a type of curtain | Yes, but only when you are cooking bread
exercise: «The Qing men had to wear the queue, a shaved forehead with a long braid.» What is a braid in this sentence? | A curtain fabric | *Hair divided into pieces and twisted into a long rope | A gold band on a uniform | A shoelace
test: In «Gold braid on the uniform showed the officer's high rank», what is braid? | Bread shaped into a loaf | A band of decorative woven thread | *A band of decorative woven thread, used on uniforms | Hair twisted into a rope
test: Which word in «Their neighbour Mrs Adeyemi comes round on Sunday nights and braids her hair» is the verb? | hair | neighbour | *braids | Sunday
exam: In which sentence does braid name the hairstyle rather than an action? | Grandad will braid the bread tomorrow | Mrs Adeyemi braids her hair on Sunday nights | *Tremaine's dad does his cornrows, which are tight braids along the head | The Woman to the Cat braided up her hair
